Model Answer:
The Board of Discipline (BOD) can impose the following sanctions if it finds a student guilty of a disciplinary offence:

(a) Issue a letter of warning to the student;  
(b) Stipulate a written undertaking by the student, including a statement on further sanctions if conditions of the undertaking are not met;  
(c) Require any restitution by the student that is accepted by the student, the complainant, and the BOD to constitute full resolution of the complaint;  
(d) Stipulate any form of service contributing to the welfare of the student community as an appropriate penalty;  
(e) Mandate counselling by the University Counselling Centre or medical treatment by a qualified medical practitioner;  
(f) Deprive the student of a pass in the whole or part of any examination, assignment, quiz, or class test;  
(g) Impose a fine not exceeding $10,000;  
(h) Expel or suspend the student permanently or temporarily, including exclusion from the University or any part of it, programmes, courses, or examinations, or deprive the student of matriculated or registered status;  
(i) Deprive the student of any degree, diploma, certificate, or other academic distinction to be awarded or previously awarded.

Only students who are expelled, suspended, fined more than $5,000, or deprived of academic awards have the right to appeal to the Appeal Committee [SOURCE 1], [SOURCE 2].
